tegory":"Quality & internal comms","d7":0.0,"d30":0.0,"share_30d_pct":0.0},{"category":"Consumer report requests","d7":6.0,"d30":60.0,"share_30d_pct":0.6},{"category":"Termwise","d7":4.0,"d30":13.0,"share_30d_pct":0.1},{"category":"Conversation","d7":0.0,"d30":1.0,"share_30d_pct":0.0}]},"by_category_by_segment_methodology":"APPROXIMATE, directional split -- does not reconcile with the Ticket Volume KPI tile above and should not be treated as exact. Built 2026-09-24 from short (3h/48h) Pylon search_issues samples per category (issue_category + user_type combined filters), applied as a ratio to the exact live by_category d30/d7 totals. Two known limitations: (1) Pylon's user_type field is freeform text, not a locked enum, so exact-match filtering misses spelling/casing variants and every ticket here is forced into PM-or-Applicant with no 'unset' bucket -- unlike the Ticket Volume KPI tile above, which uses an exact Snowflake census with a real unset bucket (~2,168 of 18,715 tickets, 30d) and is the more trustworthy number for overall PM/Applicant ticket volume. (2) short sampling windows are volatile at the category level -- adjacent 3h samples for the same category have been observed to swing by 2x+, so per-category ratios here are a rough signal, not a precise split. Most categories (9 of 15) also had sample n<10 for at least one segment. Snowflake's Support Cases BI table has no equivalent to Pylon's issue_category field (its CASE_CATEGORY/CASE_SUB_CATEGORY are Salesforce-only), so this couldn't be rebuilt with the same rigor as the KPI tile. Use for rough shape/trend only; if PM vs. Applicant category volume needs to be exact, it requires a full (non-sampled) Pylon pagination census, not yet built.","tickets":[{"ticket_id":"159031","subject":"SOP change request - 24Fifty and Union Station","category":"application_questions","priority":"medium","status":"open","agent":"Natalie Jamnik","account_name":null,"created_at":"2026-06-24T14:24:59.102Z","age":null,"is_tes":f